Then, we have the following analogue of Corollary A.2 (compare with Remark A.1).

**Corollary A.5**.: _Let \(\kappa\) be a convex quadrilateral and let \(k,\,m,\,p,\,q\) and \(\hat{\pi}\) be like in Theorem A.3. We define the operator \(\pi\in\mathcal{L}(W^{k+1\,p}(\kappa);W^{m,\,q}(\kappa))\) by:_

\[\widehat{\pi v}=\hat{\pi v}.\]

_Then there exists a positive constant \(C,\) independent of the geometry of \(\kappa,\) such that:_

\[\left\{\begin{aligned} &|v-\pi v|_{0,\,q,\kappa}\leqslant C\sigma_{ \kappa}^{2/p}h_{\kappa}^{k+1\,+\,2/q-2/p}|v|_{k+1,\,p,\kappa},\\ &|v-\pi v|_{m,\,q,\kappa}\leqslant C\sigma_{\kappa}^{4m-2\,+\,2/ p}h_{\kappa}^{k+1\,-m+\,2/q-2/p}|v|_{k+1,\,p,\kappa}& m\geqslant 1\\ &\forall v\in W^{k+1,\,p}(\kappa).\end{aligned}\right.\] (A.46)

_When \(\kappa\) belongs to a regular triangulation \(\mathcal{T}_{h}\), these two inequalities reduce to:_

\[|v-\pi v|_{m,\,q,\kappa}\leqslant Ch^{k+1-m+2/q-2/p}|v|_{k+1,\,p,\kappa}.\] (A.47)

_Remark A.3_.: For all \(m\geqslant 2,\) a simple exercise on the differentiation of compound functions shows that, unlike \([\hat{v}]_{m,\,p,\,\hat{\kappa}},\) the full seminorm \(|\hat{v}|_{m,\,p,\,\hat{\kappa}}\) satisfies a very poor upper bound in terms of \(\|v\|_{m,\,p,\kappa}.\) The trouble arises from the cross derivatives \(\hat{\sigma}^{m}\hat{v}/\partial\hat{x}_{1}^{i}\,\partial\hat{x}_{2}^{m-i}\) for \(1\leqslant i\leqslant m-1,\) because they involve (in particular) the factor \(\hat{\sigma}^{2}F_{\kappa}/\partial\hat{x}_{1}\,\partial\hat{x}_{2}\) instead of products of the form \((\partial F_{\kappa}/\partial\hat{x}_{k})(\partial F_{\kappa}/\partial\hat{x} _{l}).\) And, unless \(\kappa\) is nearly a parallelogram, \(\hat{\sigma}^{2}F_{\kappa}/\partial\hat{x}_{1}\,\partial\hat{x}_{2}\) is only of the order of \(h_{\kappa}\) whereas \((\partial F_{\kappa}/\partial\hat{x}_{k})(\partial F_{\kappa}/\partial\hat{x} _{l})\) is of the order of \(h_{\kappa}^{2}.\) This is why the seminorm \([\,.\,]_{m,\,p,\,\Omega}\) plays a vital part in deriving accurate error estimates for quadrilateral finite elements.

_Remark A.4_.: Observe that the exponent of the regularity factor \(\sigma_{\kappa}\) is much higher in (A.46) than in (A.17). This comes from the fact that the transformation \(F_{\kappa}\) is not affine.

